-- facts/js-dataflow.rofl — the value core's vocabulary and its claims.
--
-- SEPARATE FROM facts/js-kinds.rofl ON PURPOSE, and the audit is what taught
-- me: the first draft put these `handled` rows in the base file, where the
-- kinds they name are not declared, and `orphan_claim[audit]` said so — a claim
-- about a cell that does not exist. Each layer's pack declares the vocabulary
-- it names, exactly as facts/js-callgraph.rofl does for the twenty-four kinds
-- the call graph dragged in. A claim and the kind it is about travel together
-- or neither is checkable.

node_kind(js, string_literal).
node_kind(js, numeric_literal).
node_kind(js, boolean_literal).
node_kind(js, identifier).
node_kind(js, object_expression).
node_kind(js, array_expression).
node_kind(js, function_expression).
node_kind(js, arrow_function_expression).
node_kind(js, class_declaration).
node_kind(js, parenthesized_expression).
node_kind(js, tsas_expression).
node_kind(js, tsnon_null_expression).
node_kind(js, object_method).
node_kind(js, class_method).
node_kind(js, optional_call_expression).
node_kind(js, return_statement).
node_kind(js, member_expression).
node_kind(js, optional_member_expression).
node_kind(js, object_property).
node_kind(js, this_expression).

-- THE VALUE CORE, 2026-09-04 (queue item w_df_value_core). Everything above
-- moves a value between names; these are the cells that say what a value IS.
-- `r_value_core` is rules/js-dataflow.rofl's may_be_lit / may_be_node.
--
-- FIVE OF THE EIGHT THE ITEM CLAIMED. `member_expression`, `object_property`
-- and `this_expression` are NOT ticked and are not oversights: reading a
-- property off a value is a second question (the call graph answers a narrow
-- case of it in `denotes`), and `this` outside a class method has no answer
-- this layer can give. They stay open with the default reason.
handled(js, string_literal,     dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, numeric_literal,    dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, identifier,         dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, object_expression,  dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, array_expression,   dataflow, r_value_core).

-- and the three transparent wrappers, which `w_df_control_forms` claimed and
-- this item closed by accident: a parenthesis, a cast and a non-null assertion
-- change nothing about a value, so they are one rule over a table rather than
-- three cells of work. The queue notices — a claim on a shut cell is a row.
handled(js, parenthesized_expression, dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, tsas_expression,          dataflow, r_value_core).
handled(js, tsnon_null_expression,    dataflow, r_value_core).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ACROSS A CALL (queue item w_df_function_forms). `r_call_flow` is the
-- argument-into-parameter and return-value half of rules/js-dataflow.rofl.
--
-- `new_expression` is NOT ticked: its arguments reach the constructor's
-- parameters like any other call, but the VALUE of `new C()` is an instance and
-- this layer has no notion of one. That is the same cell `w_cg_new_expression`
-- is about, seen from the other side.
handled(js, function_declaration,      dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, function_expression,       dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, arrow_function_expression, dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, object_method,             dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, class_method,              dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, optional_call_expression,  dataflow, r_call_flow).
handled(js, return_statement,          dataflow, r_call_flow).

-- READING A PROPERTY OFF A VALUE, and `this` (queue item w_df_value_core,
-- finished). `r_member_value` is the member_value / selects / this half of
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl. All three used to be answered privately inside the
-- call graph's `denotes`; they are dataflow facts and they came home.
handled(js, member_expression,          dataflow, r_member_value).
handled(js, optional_member_expression, dataflow, r_member_value).
handled(js, object_property,            dataflow, r_member_value).
handled(js, this_expression,            dataflow, r_member_value).

-- CONSTRUCTION (queue item w_df_function_forms, finished). `new C()` and the
-- name `C` both reach the class node; the instance/class conflation that buys
-- is declared in rules/js-dataflow.rofl and queued as w_df_instance_vs_class.
handled(js, new_expression, dataflow, r_construct).

-- BRANCHING VALUE FORMS, 2026-09-04. `a || b`, `a && b` and `a ?? b` are the
-- same may-set union as a conditional's two branches — measured, not assumed:
-- `(box || other).get()` resolved to nothing while `(flag ? box : other).get()`
-- resolved, on the same corpus in the same run, and both reported the SAME
-- callee shape. The two forms mean the same thing to a reader and one of them
-- was invisible.
--
-- The callgraph cell for this kind stays open on purpose: the value rule is
-- what is claimed here, and `(a || b)()` as a CALL callee has no site in the
-- corpus, so a callgraph tick would be a claim nothing could check.
handled(js, logical_expression, dataflow, r_value_logical).

-- VALUES CROSSING A CONTROL CONSTRUCT, 2026-09-04 (queue item
-- w_df_control_forms). Four of the item's five cells; the fifth is below.
--
-- `conditional_expression` and `sequence_expression` needed no rule at all —
-- both were modelled when the value core landed and neither cell was ever
-- ticked, so the matrix under-reported its own coverage for a day. That is the
-- defect `unrecorded_coverage[audit]` reports at the CALLGRAPH layer and
-- nothing reports at this one; the two ticks are the fix and the missing audit
-- is a finding.
handled(js, conditional_expression, dataflow, r_value_branch).
handled(js, sequence_expression,    dataflow, r_value_branch).
-- `await` is transparent, which is a claim about the may-set and not about
-- promises; rules/js-dataflow.rofl states which case it is wrong about.
handled(js, await_expression,       dataflow, r_await_transparent).
-- for-of binds its variable to what the source hands out, and an array and a
-- generator are reached differently on purpose.
handled(js, for_of_statement,       dataflow, r_for_of).

-- ...AND `yield_expression` STAYS OPEN, with the reason named rather than
-- taking the default. `yields[flow]` models what a generator hands to its
-- consumer, which is what for-of walks — but the value of the `yield`
-- EXPRESSION itself is whatever the consumer passes to `.next(v)`, travelling
-- in the opposite direction from every other edge in this layer. It is real
-- work and it is not this item's: it needs a term for the generator protocol,
-- which also covers `gen.next(v)`, `yield*` and a generator's return value.
-- Queued as w_df_generator_protocol.
-- RETIRED 2026-09-05: the cell is `handled` and `stale_reason[audit]` named
-- this row the moment it was — an excuse outliving its cause.

-- THE CLASS AS A VALUE GREW THREE ANSWERS AND THE CELL STAYS OPEN, 2026-09-04.
-- The dataflow layer now says which methods a class has, which constructor it
-- answers with (its own or the nearest ancestor's), and which members it
-- inherits with a subclass's own declaration shadowing — the last of those
-- found by ADDING A FIXTURE rather than by reading, since the corpus had no
-- class extending another with a method to inherit.
--
-- AND NONE OF THAT TICKS THE CELL. `handled(js, class_declaration, dataflow,
-- r_class_chain)` was written here and `queue_stale[audit]` named it in one
-- run: `w_df_instance_vs_class` claims this very cell for a gap that is still
-- open — `new C()` and the name `C` both reach the class node, so an instance
-- is not told from its class. A kind-level cell at a layer with no refinement
-- column is ALL of that layer's answers about the kind, and three new ones do
-- not make it complete. The queue was the thing that knew.

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE DATAFLOW SWEEP, 2026-09-05. Twelve cells in the bucket, and the number
-- that matters is not how many were residue but how many raised a NEW question:
-- ZERO. The control-flow sweep spawned four items, the call-graph sweep two,
-- and this one none — every cell that is not residue landed on an item that
-- already existed. That is the first measured deceleration in this queue.

-- ALREADY MODELLED AND NEVER RECORDED, the third instance of the matrix
-- under-reporting its own coverage. `literal_kind(boolean_literal)` has been in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl since the value core landed; `const b = true` derives
-- `may_be_lit[flow](E, true)` at the literal, the binder and the use — three
-- rows, measured. Nothing was written for this cell; it was already true.
handled(js, boolean_literal, dataflow, r_value_literal).
-- ...and `null_literal`, added 2026-09-06 when `catch { return null; }` entered
-- the corpus with the propagation fixtures and `vocabulary_gap[audit]` named it
-- within one run — the second time in two iterations that gate has caught a kind
-- my own fixture introduced, which is the whole reason it counts rather than
-- reporting nothing.
node_kind(js, null_literal).
handled(js, null_literal, dataflow, r_value_literal).

-- UNREACHABLE UNDER THIS PARSER, and the same argument as its call-graph twin
-- rather than a new one: babel's `Import` node is what `import` names, and
-- scanners/js_ast.ts parses with `plugins: ['typescript']`, under which a
-- dynamic import arrives as a single `import_expression` and never as `Import`.
-- No corpus this scanner produces can fill the cell.
ignored(js, import, dataflow, a_babel_emits_no_import_callee).

-- ===========================================================================
-- WHAT A CLASS-SHAPED NODE DENOTES, 2026-09-05 — queue item w_df_instance_vs_class.
--
-- THE FIXTURE CAME FIRST, because the item said so: the corpus had no site
-- that could show the difference, and a split nothing can see is the shape this
-- session has been bitten by twice. `Vat` with a static `tapped` and an
-- instance `poured`, and four sites — two legal, two that are TypeErrors and
-- are wrapped so the run reaches the end. The two false edges appeared BY NAME
-- in the over-approximation list before a rule was written, and disappeared
-- after: `useMethodOnClass -> poured` and `useStaticOnInstance -> tapped`.
--
-- THE FACT WAS ON THE STORE ALL ALONG. `ast_attr(M, static, true|false)` is
-- emitted for every class method and no rule had ever read it.
handled(js, class_declaration, dataflow, r_class_receiver).

-- AND THE THIRD FACE WAS ALREADY MODELLED, which only a site could show.
-- `super.hold(n)` — a member read on a receiver that is a different class from
-- the one you are in — resolves, and the runtime takes the edge: UNSOUND stayed
-- 0 and over-approximation stayed 3 when the site was added. The value was
-- there; the corpus had no site. Sixth instance of the matrix under-reporting.
handled(js, super, dataflow, r_member_value).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE GENERATOR PROTOCOL, 2026-09-05 — queue item w_df_generator_protocol.
--
-- `yield_expression x dataflow` carried `not_yet` with a reason that named its
-- own difficulty: the value of a `yield` EXPRESSION arrives from the consumer's
-- `.next(v)`, travelling from a call site into the middle of a suspended body,
-- which is the opposite direction from every other edge in this layer. Both
-- directions are now modelled — what a generator YIELDS was already there, and
-- what it RECEIVES is the other half.
--
-- MEASURED AGAINST V8 FIRST, and all three claims came from the run rather than
-- from the spec: `g.next(f)` makes the yield evaluate to `f`; `yield* inner()`
-- passes the sent value THROUGH to inner's own yield; and the delegating
-- expression evaluates to what inner RETURNED, which is the one place a
-- generator's `return` is read at all.
handled(js, yield_expression, dataflow, r_generator_protocol).

-- ...AND THE CALL-GRAPH HALF FALLS OUT OF THE SAME RULE, which `false_done`
-- was right to insist on: the item owned both cells and only one had been
-- ticked. A yield expression in CALLEE position - `(yield 'go')(3)` - resolves
-- because it now HAS a value. Asserted by a site the runtime takes, not
-- claimed: UNSOUND stayed 0 when it was added.
handled(js, yield_expression, s_yield_result, callgraph, r_generator_protocol).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE FOUR KINDS THE FRAME ADDED, 2026-09-06 (w_vocabulary_frame). The kind is
-- re-declared here because a file that makes a claim declares the kind — the
-- convention w_vocabulary_home exists to question, and the one in force.
node_kind(js, binary_expression).
node_kind(js, unary_expression).
node_kind(js, class_body).
node_kind(js, template_element).

ignored(js, binary_expression, dataflow, a_a_primitive_is_not_a_node).
ignored(js, unary_expression, dataflow, a_a_primitive_is_not_a_node).
ignored(js, class_body, dataflow, a_a_statement_carries_no_value).
ignored(js, template_element, dataflow, a_a_quasi_is_not_an_expression).

-- THE MODULE BOUNDARY, 2026-09-07 (w_cg_module_boundary). An imported name MAY
-- BE the function the exporting module declares under that name; the export
-- declaration names a value rather than carrying one of its own. Entry 9 in
-- rules/js-dataflow.rofl.
handled(js, import_declaration,       dataflow, r_imported_name).
handled(js, import_specifier,         dataflow, r_imported_name).
handled(js, export_named_declaration, dataflow, r_imported_name).

-- TEN TYPE NODES, 2026-09-07 (w_type_surface). A type annotation is erased
-- before anything runs, so it holds no value for this layer to carry. NOT the
-- same question as `which prototype does this VALUE have`, which is where the
-- deferral put them and which no annotation in the corpus answers.
node_kind(js, tsfunction_type).
node_kind(js, tsnull_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsnumber_keyword).
node_kind(js, tsstring_keyword).
node_kind(js, tstype_alias_declaration).
node_kind(js, tstype_annotation).
node_kind(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation).
node_kind(js, tstype_query).
node_kind(js, tstype_reference).
node_kind(js, tsunion_type).

ignored(js, tsfunction_type,                dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsnull_keyword,                 dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsnumber_keyword,               dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsstring_keyword,               dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_alias_declaration,       dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_annotation,              dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_parameter_instantiation, dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_query,                   dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tstype_reference,               dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).
ignored(js, tsunion_type,                   dataflow, a_type_only_no_runtime_value).

-- DECLARED HERE AND ABSENT FROM THE CORPUS, with the reason written down;
-- `kind_unexercised[audit]` demands one, and a row belongs where its kind is
-- declared.
kind_absent_ok(parenthesized_expression, a_babel_emits_no_parenthesized_node).

-- ...and the two kinds those verdicts are about are declared HERE too, which
-- `orphan_claim[audit]` demanded within one run: a world loading this pack
-- and not facts/js-modules.rofl carried a claim about a cell it had no kind
-- for. Third time this convention has bitten in one session — a row belongs
-- in the pack that declares its subject, and a verdict declares the kind.
node_kind(js, import_specifier).
node_kind(js, export_named_declaration).

-- THE OTHER TWO SPECIFIERS AND THE DEFAULT EXPORT, 2026-09-07. A namespace
-- import binds the module object — the other file's `program` node — and a
-- default import binds the one unnamed export. `export default` had a node
-- and NO CONSUMER until alpha.mjs got one that beta.mjs imports; a form the
-- harness calls is not a form the model can be checked on.
handled(js, import_namespace_specifier, dataflow, r_imported_name).
handled(js, import_default_specifier,   dataflow, r_imported_name).
handled(js, export_default_declaration, dataflow, r_imported_name).
-- ...and the kinds, declared here because their verdicts are.
node_kind(js, import_namespace_specifier).
node_kind(js, import_default_specifier).
node_kind(js, export_default_declaration).
node_kind(js, export_all_declaration).
-- THE EXCUSE IS GONE BECAUSE THE SITE ARRIVED: gamma.mjs re-exports two
-- modules, so `kind_absent_ok(export_all_declaration, a_no_corpus_site)` would
-- be stale here and in the two other packs that declare the kind — the home
-- rule again, from the direction of retirement rather than of addition.
handled(js, export_all_declaration,     dataflow, r_reexported_name).
